    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "In this thesis, I address acquisitions in the technological market from January 1, 2014, to December 31, 2018, initiated by Alphabet (Google), Amazon, Meta (Facebook), Apple, and Microsoft (collectively referred to as GAFAM). Prior literature on technological market acquisitions focuses on antitrust regulation and motivators such as competition elimination, human capital retention, and vertical expansion. While some literature analyzes GAFAM’s acquisition strategies, it often relies on case studies and lacks broader analysis. My thesis aims to understand why GAFAM pursues specific types of acquisitions and their hidden motivators. I gather acquisition data primarily from PitchBook and LinkedIn to create a data set and employ logistic regression models to identify factors correlating with different types of acquisitions (e.g., those driven by human capital retention). I conclude by summarizing my results, connecting them to prior literature, and proposing future research avenues.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Aponte, B. (2024). Tech Giants and Trojan Horses: The Hidden Motivators Behind Tech Acquisitions. Stanford Digital Repository. Available at https://purl.stanford.edu/bh083qn2873. https://doi.org/10.25740/bh083qn2873.",
            "type": "preferred citation",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        }
